From 129326d02fa74b9d9985d2a13d1f188cc6845357 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 03:55:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Twelve bosses keep a kill count, and the varp was protected
 all along

Backlog item 10, surveyed three rounds ago and unblocked since. Kill one of twelve bosses and the
count goes up; the counts live in a window of their own, opened from a row appended to the quest
list.

THE TWELVE ARE MEASURED, NOT RECALLED. Every attackable npc in the tree was listed by its own
vislevel - 256 sit at 100 or above - and these are what is left once the slayer superiors (their
own system), the fight cave's lesser waves and the ordinary high-level monsters are set aside:
TzTok-Jad, K'ril, Graardor, Zilyana, Kree'arra, the Kalphite Queen, the Chaos Elemental, all three
Dagannoth kings, the King Black Dragon and the Giant Mole.

The Kalphite Queen is counted on the FLYING form. The first form does not die - which is why its
drop table is hooked there too, with a comment saying so - and counting both would double every
kill. A check holds it.

EVERY DISPLAY NAME COMES OUT OF THE BOSS'S OWN .npc RECORD, not out of the spec, so the window
cannot disagree with what the player sees over the monster's head. "King black dragon" with a
lower-case black is the cache's own spelling.

HOOKED IN [proc,npc_death], which is the one funnel every death in the game passes through - and
the only one available, because a second [ai_queue3] on an npc that already has one is a hard build
error and ALL TWELVE of these already have theirs for a drop table. ~barrows_potential hangs off
the same place for the same reason and was the template for the whole shape of this.

CREDIT IS npc_findhero, the test every drop table in this tree opens with, so a count goes to
exactly the player the loot went to. %npc_aggressive_player is the LAST attacker and is a different
thing. Slayer deliberately SPLITS a task kill when those two differ; a kill count is not split,
because two players each being told they killed the same boss is a wrong number rather than a
generous one.

TWENTY-FOUR CASES IS WHAT "THE ENGINE CANNOT PICK A VARP AT RUNTIME" COSTS. engine.rs2 has no varp
commands and no enum outputs one, so there is no %vars[$i]: the increment is a generated switch_int
over twelve cases and the read-back is another. What CAN be a table is: boss_kill_index maps the
dying npc to a slot and boss_kill_name maps a slot to its name, both enums, and the comment in that
file says why the two halves look different.

FOUR BUILDS WERE SPENT ON THE WRONG CAUSE, and the fix was one line in a varp declaration.

    ERROR: Attempt to access corrupted pointer [ 'p_active_player' ]

at the call site in npc_death.rs2. VARPS ARE PROTECTED BY DEFAULT, and writing a protected varp
needs a pointer an npc-context death does not have, whatever npc_findhero returns. Moving the guard
to the call site changed nothing. Inlining the switch changed nothing. Dropping the queue changed
nothing. Calling it before ~check_progress_task changed nothing. Reducing the proc to one plain
varp write - byte-for-byte the shape of ~barrows_potential, which works - changed nothing.

The difference was never in the script. %barrows_killed_monster is a varbit on [barrows_kills],
which carries protect=no, and skill_guide.varp explains the same rule for [if_button] triggers in a
comment that has been there for rounds. The twelve varps carry protect=no now and boss_kills.varp
says all of this at length, because the next person to write a varp for an npc-context write should
find the answer in a file and not in four builds.

AND THE GUARD WENT MISSING FOR ONE BUILD while that was being chased - and THE BUILD WENT GREEN
WITH IT GONE, because protect=no had by then made the write legal. Without npc_findhero the counter
is written for whoever the death context happens to be pointing at. It is back, it is the first
statement in the proc, and it is checked by name and checked first for exactly that reason.

THE WINDOW is its own, Corey's choice when asked. Twelve rows, name left and count right, filled by
if_settext when it opens rather than transmitted - so the varps stay server-side and there is
nothing to keep in sync. The counts start at "0" rather than blank, because an empty row reads as a
broken window. if_settext is (component, string) and the first version had it the other way round,
which is a build error rather than a surprise.

It opens from ONE ROW appended to questlist.if's scrolling layer, with an
[if_button,questlist:boss_kills] behind it - the same idiom all 129 quest rows already use to open
their journals. The generator inserts that row and grows the layer's scroll to reach it without
owning the file, the way genxplock.py inserts into stats.if.

That insert was not idempotent on its first re-run: the removal regex wanted a blank line after the
block, and the block is the last thing in the file. The packer caught it - "Layer questlist:com_0
already has boss_kills as a child" - which is the same fault genxplock.py had on stats.if. Two
consecutive runs now leave one row, and a check counts them.

32 mutations, each caught by its own check, none loose and none skipped, and this harness has the
anchor uniqueness rule. Two of the checks were too weak and the mutations proved it:

  - the default case was tested with an `in`, so a mutation that moved it to the FRONT of the
    switch walked through - and a default ahead of the numbered cases is a switch where none of
    them is ever reached. It has to be last now.
  - the window's rows were counted by name PREFIX, so renaming count7 to count7x kept the total at
    twelve while the opener pointed at a component that no longer existed. Exact names now.

ONE OTHER BATTERY'S CHECK WENT RED ON CORRECT CODE, and it was right to. gamemode_battery asserted
xp_locked was the LAST line in varp.pack - a proxy for the thing it cares about, which is that a
varp is APPENDED and never inserted, because a varp id is part of what a save file means. Twelve
new varps at 1178 and up made the proxy false while the property held. It pins the id instead, and
xp_rate's beside it, and gamemode_mutate's label moved with it.

CI gets both new tools. The four batteries that are still not in it - gamemode, barrows,
leprechaun, follower - are noted in the workflow and the backlog, unchanged.

